.TH XTC_BATCH 3 "2026" "libxtc" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
xtc_batch_create, xtc_batch_create_async, xtc_batch_submit,
xtc_batch_submit_mt, xtc_batch_cancel, xtc_batch_cancel_mt,
xtc_batch_resume, xtc_batch_resume_mt,
xtc_batch_close, xtc_batch_destroy, xtc_batch_get_stats,
xtc_batch_get_async_stats,
xtc_batch_loop_for_device, xtc_batch_router_create,
xtc_batch_router_destroy, xtc_batch_route \- bounded continuous batching
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <xtc_batch.h>

int xtc_batch_create(xtc_loop_t *loop, const xtc_batch_opts_t *opts,
                    xtc_batch_t **out);
int xtc_batch_create_async(xtc_loop_t *loop,
                    const xtc_batch_async_opts_t *opts, xtc_batch_t **out);
int xtc_batch_submit(xtc_batch_t *group, void *request, uint64_t charge,
                    uint64_t *out_handle);
int xtc_batch_submit_mt(xtc_batch_t *group, void *request, uint64_t charge,
                    uint64_t *out_handle);
int xtc_batch_cancel(xtc_batch_t *group, uint64_t handle);
int xtc_batch_cancel_mt(xtc_batch_t *group, uint64_t handle);
int xtc_batch_resume(xtc_batch_t *group, uint64_t handle);
int xtc_batch_resume_mt(xtc_batch_t *group, uint64_t handle);
int xtc_batch_close(xtc_batch_t *group, enum xtc_batch_close_mode mode);
int xtc_batch_destroy(xtc_batch_t *group);
int xtc_batch_get_stats(xtc_batch_t *group, xtc_batch_stats_t *out);
int xtc_batch_get_async_stats(xtc_batch_t *group,
                    xtc_batch_async_stats_t *out);
int xtc_batch_loop_for_device(xtc_exec_t *exec,
                    const xtc_accel_dev_t *dev, int *out_loop_idx);
int xtc_batch_router_create(xtc_batch_t **groups, int n,
                    xtc_batch_router_t **out);
void xtc_batch_router_destroy(xtc_batch_router_t *router);
xtc_batch_t *xtc_batch_route(xtc_batch_router_t *router, uint64_t key);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A group owns a pinned driver process on the supplied loop. Each synchronous
step callback receives one immutable vector of ready members, not one call per
request. Finished members leave; waiting members refill at subsequent iteration
boundaries. No scheduling policy is required. The API is available when built
with --enable-sched-policy; disabled builds return XTC_E_NOSYS.
.PP
Initialize the size and version fields of options and statistics descriptors
to sizeof the descriptor and XTC_BATCH_VERSION. The descriptor is copied;
callback context and submitted request pointers remain borrowed.
Options specify capacity, max_active, resource_capacity, sched_class,
step, complete, and user. Both callbacks are mandatory.
Capacity must be at least max_active, and both must be positive.
Resource capacity must be positive. A NULL class selects default scheduling;
a supplied class must belong to this loop and cannot be registered to another
batch group. Ordinary non-batch processes may share that class. The class
registration remains reserved until successful group destruction.
.PP
Create is allowed only before the loop's first run or simulation step.
Setup must be externally serialized against execution. All storage is allocated
before publishing the driver; create failure publishes no group. Allocation
size arithmetic is checked, including on 32-bit targets.
.PP
Submit accepts a fixed maximum resource charge in 1..resource_capacity.
Slots include both waiting and active requests. Admission to the active set is
strict first-come, first-served: a younger request cannot bypass an older one
whose charge does not fit. READY and BLOCKED requests both occupy active slots
and retain their resource reservation until their outcome becomes terminal.
Submission itself reserves a slot, not active resource units.
Full or closed groups return XTC_E_AGAIN. Failed submission causes no callback
and leaves the output handle unchanged. Request may be NULL if the application
callback supports it.
.PP
Handles are nonzero, monotonically issued uint64_t identities, never reused
within a group. Exhaustion returns XTC_E_RANGE rather than wrapping. Handles
must be paired with the group that issued them; they are not transferable.
.SH CALLBACKS
The step callback has signature:
.nf
int step(const xtc_batch_item_t *items, xtc_batch_result_t *results,
         size_t count, void *user);
.fi
Each item contains handle and request. Results are initialized to UNSET before
each invocation. Set each state to XTC_BATCH_CONTINUE, XTC_BATCH_DONE,
XTC_BATCH_BLOCKED, or XTC_BATCH_ERROR. ERROR requires an existing nonzero
XTC_E_* error in its error field; other states ignore error.
Omitted or invalid results fail that member with XTC_E_INTERNAL.
A nonzero step return fails the entire current vector with that existing
XTC_E_* error, or XTC_E_INTERNAL for an invalid error value. Neither vectors
nor callback side effects are retried automatically.
.PP
The terminal callback has signature:
.nf
void complete(uint64_t handle, void *request, int outcome, void *user);
.fi
Outcome is XTC_OK for DONE, XTC_E_ABORTED for cancellation, or the recorded
failure. The request borrow lasts through callback return. Completion invocation
is recorded before entering user code; unexpected driver exit never starts the
same notification twice. Recorded terminal outcomes, including DONE, survive
unexpected driver exit. Other outstanding work fails with XTC_E_INTERNAL
(or XTC_E_ABORTED if already cancelled). Such exit closes the group.
.PP
Callbacks run outside runtime bookkeeping and may yield, but must return in
bounded time for progress. While any callback is in flight, ALL mutators on
that group return XTC_E_DEADLK, including peer processes scheduled while the
callback is suspended. Destroy is a mutator. Read-only statistics are allowed
and report committed state, not partially written result words.
Cancellation is masked across callbacks through result recording and completion
accounting. A nonreturning or crashing callback cannot be guaranteed to finish
its own application cleanup; exactly-once invocation does not imply successful
application processing. Arbitrary nonlocal jumps out of the driver are unsupported.
.SH CANCELLATION AND RESUME
Cancel records a request for terminal cancellation at a boundary. A cancellation
recorded before the next vector excludes the request. Already recorded DONE
wins over later cancellation. Stale handles return XTC_E_INVAL after terminal
notification. Duplicate cancellation before notification is harmless.
.PP
Resume changes a live BLOCKED request to READY. Duplicate resume on READY is
harmless. WAITING and terminal requests are not resumable and return XTC_E_INVAL.
Resume does not release or reacquire a blocked request's fixed resource charge.
.SH ASYNC OFFLOAD
.B xtc_batch_create_async
builds a group whose step is a split
.I submit / collect
pair instead of one synchronous step, so an iteration may launch device or
pool work and be awaited WITHOUT stalling the driver or the other members.
The group is otherwise identical: submit, cancel, resume, close, destroy,
and get_stats behave as for a synchronous group, and continuous batching's
variable-length completion is preserved across the asynchronous boundary.
Options carry capacity, max_active, resource_capacity, exec_timeout_ns,
sched_class, submit, collect, complete, an optional cancel_hint, and user.
submit, collect, and complete are mandatory.
exec_timeout_ns must not be negative; 0 means no engine timeout.
Initialize size and version as for the synchronous options.
Submission into an async group uses the same xtc_batch_submit path and the
same handle rules.
.PP
At each iteration boundary the driver builds the ready vector and calls
.nf
int submit(const xtc_batch_item_t *items, size_t count, void *user,
           xtc_batch_exec_t *out_exec);
.fi
The application launches ONE operation covering all count members and fills
out_exec with a completion descriptor whose kind is one of:
.TP
.B XTC_BATCH_EXEC_SYNC
The work was done inline in submit; the engine does not park and calls
collect at once (lets a CPU group use the async API uniformly).
.TP
.B XTC_BATCH_EXEC_FENCE
A pollable completion fence fd in out_exec->fence_fd (sync_file / dma-fence /
eventfd), awaited through the same loop readiness path as xtc_accel_wait_fence.
The application owns the fd; the engine never dups or closes it and simply
stops waiting on teardown.
NATIVE ONLY: the simulator has no fd-readiness hook, so a FENCE group is not
sim-reachable (use FUTURE for deterministic coverage).
.TP
.B XTC_BATCH_EXEC_FUTURE
An xtc_future_t in out_exec->future that the application resolves from any
thread when its own completion fires.
Pass the READ half (the future); the engine takes responsibility for consuming
it exactly once, via the normal wait on completion or by discarding it on
timeout or teardown.
The application keeps the PROMISE half and resolves or drops it; it must NOT
also wait on or free the future handle it handed over.
This half-and-half split keeps the token leak-free and double-free-free across
teardown.
FUTURE is the sim-deterministic path.
.TP
.B XTC_BATCH_EXEC_BLOCKING
A synchronous closure out_exec->run(out_exec->arg) run on the xtc_blocking
pool; the driver parks while a pool thread runs it (coarse offload, one pool
thread held).
.PP
A nonzero submit return fails that iteration's members (no operation is
outstanding).
On XTC_OK the engine moves the ready members in flight, awaits the token, then
calls
.nf
int collect(const xtc_batch_item_t *items, xtc_batch_result_t *results,
            size_t count, int exec_status, void *user);
.fi
on the driver with the SAME frozen vector and the raw exec status.
collect reads device outputs and sets each result state (CONTINUE, DONE,
BLOCKED, or ERROR) exactly as a synchronous step would, so one member emitting
end-of-stream becomes DONE while the rest CONTINUE.
collect reads only the vectors it is handed, never live group state.
It runs exactly once per outstanding operation, including on teardown, so the
application can always free that operation's device buffers.
.PP
exec_status is a POSITIVE engine sentinel, distinct from an xtc_err a device
may report, so the two are never confused:
.B XTC_BATCH_EXEC_OK
(the token fired),
.B XTC_BATCH_EXEC_TIMEOUT
(exec_timeout_ns elapsed first), or
.B XTC_BATCH_EXEC_ABORTED
(the group was cancel-closed or torn down while the operation was
outstanding).
The engine never auto-retries a side-effecting operation; collect decides,
returning CONTINUE to keep a member and resubmit next iteration, or failing it.
.PP
The per-member device-output location and per-sequence cache slot are NOT new
struct fields: the request pointer the application submitted is its
per-sequence handle and carries both.
complete(handle, request, outcome, user) firing is the application's cue to
release that sequence's resources.
libxtc owns no model state; the abstraction stops at the completion token.
.PP
CANCELLATION AT A BOUNDARY.
A cancel (owner loop) on a member whose operation is outstanding is ACCEPTED,
not refused: it only records the cancellation and does not interrupt the
operation mid-flight.
It is consumed at the completion boundary, where collect still runs (device
buffer freed) and the member then ends XTC_E_ABORTED exactly once.
If cancel_hint is set it fires when a cancel lands on an in-flight member, so
the application may signal its own runtime.
Cancellation is iteration-granular, never mid-kernel.
.PP
EXACTLY ONCE ACROSS TEARDOWN.
Each in-flight member is finalized by exactly one call, whether the operation
completed normally, timed out, was cancelled, or the group was destroyed while
the driver was parked on the token.
A forced teardown runs collect once with XTC_BATCH_EXEC_ABORTED before
finalizing, so a device buffer is never leaked and a member is never completed
twice.
.PP
.B xtc_batch_get_async_stats
reports offloads, offload_timeouts, offload_errors, mt_queued, and
mt_rejections through a separate
xtc_batch_async_stats_t (size and version checked like the synchronous
statistics); the synchronous xtc_batch_stats_t is unchanged.
On a synchronous group these counters are zero.
.SH CROSS-LOOP ADMISSION
.B xtc_batch_submit_mt,
.B xtc_batch_cancel_mt,
and
.B xtc_batch_resume_mt
are callable from ANY loop or a plain OS thread, unlike the owner-loop-only
xtc_batch_submit/cancel/resume. They are enabled by setting mt_queue_cap > 0
in
.B xtc_batch_async_opts_t;
a group created with mt_queue_cap == 0 (or a synchronous group created with
xtc_batch_create, whose options cannot carry the field) returns XTC_E_NOSYS
from these calls. Cross-loop admission is orthogonal to the synchronous-versus-
async step: an mt-enabled async group with a plain SYNC step works the same.
.PP
mt_queue_cap bounds a lock-free MPSC admission ring and a preallocated pool of
admission records, both charged through the loop's resource accountant at
create. The three mt calls allocate nothing: they claim a record from the
pool, stamp a handle, and push onto the ring; a full ring or exhausted pool
returns XTC_E_AGAIN and counts mt_rejections. mt_queue_cap (pending hand-ins)
is distinct from capacity (admitted live requests).
.PP
HANDLE RETURNED IMMEDIATELY, ADMISSION AT A BOUNDARY.
.B xtc_batch_submit_mt
stamps a group-global monotonic handle and returns it at once, so the caller
has a usable handle before the request is admitted. The driver drains the ring
at its next iteration boundary and only then creates the waiting slot and
subjects it to the usual capacity, resource, and FCFS admission. A hand-in
that passes the ring but cannot yet be admitted waits in the group's normal
waiting state; it is not rejected after the handle is issued. Handles from the
owner-loop and mt paths share one monotonic space and are never reused.
.PP
A CANCEL IS NEVER LOST.
Because the handle is stamped before the record is pushed, a cancel issued by
a DIFFERENT producer than the one that submitted can reach the ring BEFORE its
submit. The driver handles this: a cancel or resume whose target slot does not
yet exist is held in a bounded early-control set and applied the moment the
matching submit creates the slot. A pending cancel aborts the member before it
ever runs (its step/collect are never called for it); a pending resume on a
fresh waiting slot is a no-op. Because handles are never reused, an
early-control entry can match only its own later submit. A cancel is therefore
never lost regardless of producer or ring order.
.PP
CROSS-LOOP RESULTS (REPLY-FUTURE PATTERN).
Completion still runs the group's complete callback on the DRIVER loop; the
engine does not invent a cross-loop result channel. An mt submitter that needs
the result on its own loop passes a request that carries an xtc_future_t
(created with xtc_future_new_pair) which the complete callback resolves with
xtc_promise_set; the submitter awaits that future on its own loop. The request
pointer is the consumer's per-sequence handle and carries whatever outbound
state (device-output location, KV-cache slot id) the application needs.
.SH OWNERSHIP AND SHUTDOWN
Mutation is permitted during externally serialized pre-run setup and, once
started, only from the actual owner loop. Send commands to an owner process
using existing messaging APIs for cross-loop submission. A physical thread
identity is not a loop identity, especially under deterministic simulation.
.PP
XTC_BATCH_DRAIN stops admission and waits for all accepted requests to finish;
BLOCKED requests require explicit resume or cancellation. XTC_BATCH_CANCEL
also cancels all nonterminal requests. Close is idempotent and may escalate
DRAIN to CANCEL, never downgrade it. Close always signals boundary work,
including when the driver is empty or all members are BLOCKED.
.PP
The required lifetime order is close, run until the driver ends, destroy,
then loop or executor finalization. Even closing an empty group before its
first run requires execution of its driver. Stopping a loop or executor is
not draining: destroy returns XTC_E_AGAIN while the driver remains present.
After a stopped run, route close through an owner process and run again.
.PP
With cross-loop producers the same order holds and extends to the ring. A
producer calling
.B xtc_batch_submit_mt
concurrently with close is best-effort: the admission ring is not closed by
.B xtc_batch_close
(only destroy closes it), so a racing hand-in may be accepted and return a
handle, then dropped at the next driver drain (counted in rejections, with no
completion callback), or it may lose a full ring and get XTC_E_AGAIN. An
AGAIN-on-closed result is not promised on the producer side. Freedom from a
freed-memory access is not a property the ring enforces across teardown; it
rests on the caller obligation below. The admission ring and record pool are
closed and freed only in destroy, after the driver is gone, and the group heap
itself is not freed until then. The caller must ensure no producer is still
issuing mt calls when destroy runs (the owner-outlives-group discipline); the
loop that owns the group must outlive every loop that submits to it.
.PP
Destroy requires a closed group, no borrowed requests or callback, and driver
absence from the process table. An exit hook flag or alive==0 is insufficient:
other exit hooks may still be parked. Destroy and statistics additionally allow
externally synchronized quiescent calls after executor return, with the loop
still allocated. These are not general thread-safe operations; a NULL current
loop does not prove quiescence. Calling loop/exec fini with a live group violates
the lifetime contract and does not promise automatic terminal delivery.
.PP
Normal shutdown uses batch_close, not an asynchronous driver kill. An idle
notify waiter need not wake on kill alone. A later normal batch signal permits
masked waiter unlink before pending kill delivery. There is no hidden polling
thread, periodic wake, real clock read, or unseeded randomness in the engine.
.SH PLACEMENT
The placement helpers are advisory glue, not a scheduler. There is no
automatic migration of a live group: a group stays on its creation loop.
They only help a consumer choose a loop at create time and shard across a
fixed set of groups it created itself. They are available only when built
with --enable-sched-policy; disabled builds return XTC_E_NOSYS
(and
.B xtc_batch_loop_for_device
still writes 0 to out_loop_idx).
.PP
.B xtc_batch_loop_for_device
maps a probed accelerator device (xtc_accel_probe, see xtc_accel(3)) to the
executor loop index whose carrier thread runs on the same NUMA node as the
device, best-effort. It reads the device's NUMA node from static sysfs
topology:
for a renderD* node from /sys/class/drm/<name>/device/numa_node, for an
accel* node from /sys/class/accel/<name>/device/numa_node (dev->name is the
short label, e.g. "renderD128" or "accel0"). On success it writes a valid
loop index in [0, xtc_exec_n_loops) to out_loop_idx and returns XTC_OK. When
the device numa_node is -1 or unknown, the executor's loops record no known
node (for example when the helper is called before the executor has run, so
no carrier has recorded its node yet), or the host reports a single NUMA
node, it writes 0 (the loop-0 fallback) and still returns XTC_OK: absence of
topology is not an error, matching xtc_accel's convention. It never returns
an out-of-range index. A NULL argument returns XTC_E_INVAL. The read is a
one-shot static-topology probe and touches no sim-nondeterministic source,
so it is safe to call under the deterministic simulator; it is not meant for
a per-iteration hot path.
.PP
.B xtc_batch_router_create,
.B xtc_batch_route,
and
.B xtc_batch_router_destroy
are a keyed-shard router: N groups plus a modulo, nothing more. The caller
creates one group per shard loop (xtc_batch_create or xtc_batch_create_async
per loop, typically placed with
.B xtc_batch_loop_for_device)
and hands the array to
.B xtc_batch_router_create,
which copies the pointer array (it borrows the groups; it does not own them)
and returns an opaque router.
.B xtc_batch_route
returns groups[key % n], a stable key-to-shard mapping; the same key always
returns the same group. The router does not submit: the caller does
xtc_batch_submit (or xtc_batch_submit_mt) on the group that
.B xtc_batch_route
returns. The router adds no cross-group runtime coupling.
.B xtc_batch_router_destroy
frees only the router, never the groups; the caller owns and must separately
close and destroy each group on its owner loop, following the normal batch
lifetime contract above.
.B xtc_batch_router_create
returns XTC_E_INVAL on a NULL array/out or n <= 0 (or a NULL group in the
array), XTC_E_NOMEM on allocation failure, and XTC_E_NOSYS in a disabled
build;
.B xtc_batch_route
returns NULL on a NULL router.
.SH STATISTICS
The snapshot includes queued, ready, blocked, reserved_units, iterations,
completed, cancelled, failed, rejections, max_vector, and total_members.
Queued means waiting for admission; ready and blocked count active members.
Iteration and membership totals measure vector calls and their occupancy.
Terminal counters advance after completion delivery returns (or after an
interrupted delivery is retired). Rejections count invalid or unsuccessful
submissions after owner/callback validation. Cumulative counters saturate at
UINT64_MAX. Per-request timing belongs in application instrumentation.
.SH ERRORS
XTC_E_INVAL indicates malformed arguments, descriptor size, foreign ownership,
foreign class, invalid close mode, or an unavailable handle.
XTC_E_VERSION indicates an unsupported descriptor version.
XTC_E_RANGE indicates charge, allocation arithmetic, or handle exhaustion.
XTC_E_AGAIN indicates full/closed admission, creation after first execution,
or destruction before shutdown has completed.
XTC_E_DEADLK indicates mutation during a callback.
XTC_E_NOMEM indicates allocation failure during create.
